W={
'c1w1':[(L2,'When public budgets are under pressure, funding for the arts is often regarded as an easy target. However, there are compelling reasons for governments to continue supporting culture, notably through grants to individual artists and arts education in schools.\n\nGrants to individual artists allow creative work to be produced that the market alone would not support. Experimental theatre, contemporary music and poetry rarely generate enough income to sustain their creators, yet they often shape the culture of the future. Critics argue that artists should not depend on public money, and there is some truth in this: grants can encourage work that appeals to funding committees rather than audiences. Nevertheless, without some form of support, only those with private wealth could afford to pursue an artistic career.\n\nArts education, by contrast, benefits society as a whole. Every child, regardless of background, gains the opportunity to discover a talent or simply develop the confidence that comes from performing or creating. These skills – creativity, collaboration, self-expression – are increasingly valued in the workplace.\n\nOf the two, I believe education is the more important priority. Grants support a relatively small number of professionals, whereas arts education reaches millions of young people and creates the audiences, and indeed the artists, of tomorrow. If governments must choose, investing in schools is likely to produce the broader and more lasting benefit, although ideally a modest grant system should be maintained alongside it to ensure that talent from all backgrounds can flourish.'),
 (L3,'It has become fashionable in some quarters to dismiss public spending on the arts as a luxury that hard-pressed governments can ill afford. Such a view, however, underestimates the extent to which a nation\'s cultural life depends on deliberate investment. Two forms of support – funding museums and galleries, and subsidising individual artists – illustrate the dilemma particularly well.\n\nPublicly funded museums are, in many respects, the cornerstone of cultural democracy. Free admission, where it exists, has been shown to transform visitor numbers, opening collections to schoolchildren, pensioners and families who might never otherwise cross the threshold. The objection that free museums chiefly benefit the educated middle classes is not without merit, yet it argues for better outreach rather than for withdrawing funding altogether.\n\nGrants to individual artists raise thornier questions. Supporters maintain that they protect creative risk-taking from the tyranny of the market; detractors counter that they foster dependency and reward those adept at filling in application forms rather than those with genuine talent.\n\nWeighing the two, I am persuaded that museums represent the more valuable use of limited funds. They preserve a shared heritage, serve as places of learning and reflection, and – crucially – remain accessible to everyone, rather than benefiting a select few. That said, the most enlightened cultural policies recognise that heritage and innovation are not rivals: today\'s experimental work, if it is allowed to emerge at all, may well become tomorrow\'s museum piece.')],
'c1w2':[(L2,'As populations age across much of the world, governments are being forced to rethink how they support older citizens. Of the many challenges this creates, the sustainability of pensions and the rising cost of healthcare are arguably the most urgent.\n\nPension systems in many countries were designed when people lived only a few years beyond retirement. Today, retirement can last two or three decades, and a shrinking working population must fund an ever-growing number of pensioners. Raising the retirement age is the most obvious solution, but it is deeply unpopular and particularly unfair to those in physically demanding jobs.\n\nHealthcare costs present a similar problem. Older people are more likely to live with chronic conditions requiring long-term treatment, and hospitals are already under severe strain. Without reform, waiting times will lengthen and the quality of care is likely to decline.\n\nIn my view, healthcare should be addressed first. While pension reform is undoubtedly necessary, its effects are gradual and can be planned over many years. A failing healthcare system, by contrast, has immediate and sometimes fatal consequences. Moreover, investment in preventive care – encouraging exercise, improving diet and detecting illness early – would allow people to remain healthy and active for longer, reducing costs and enabling many to work later in life if they wish. In this sense, addressing healthcare effectively would also ease the pressure on pensions, making it the more strategic priority.'),
 (L3,'The demographic shift towards an older population is frequently framed as a looming crisis. While this may be an exaggeration, it undeniably presents governments with difficult choices, of which the funding of pensions and the problem of loneliness are among the most pressing.\n\nThe pensions dilemma is essentially one of arithmetic. As the ratio of workers to retirees declines, maintaining current levels of support will require higher taxes, a later retirement age, or both. Each option carries political risk, which perhaps explains why so many governments have postponed meaningful reform for as long as possible.\n\nLoneliness, though less quantifiable, is no less serious. A growing body of research links chronic isolation to depression, cognitive decline and heart disease, with some studies suggesting its effects are comparable to smoking fifteen cigarettes a day. As families become more geographically dispersed, an increasing number of older people may go days without meaningful human contact.\n\nIt is tempting to prioritise pensions simply because the financial stakes are so visible. I would contend, however, that tackling loneliness should come first, precisely because it is so often neglected and because the solutions are relatively inexpensive. Community centres, befriending schemes and intergenerational housing projects require modest investment yet can transform lives – and, by keeping older people healthier, reduce demands on public services. Pension reform cannot be avoided indefinitely, but a society that ignores the isolation of its older members has, in a very real sense, already failed them.')],
'c1w3':[(L2,'Report on student clubs\n\nPurpose\nThis report assesses the effectiveness of the college\'s student clubs and proposes measures to increase participation. The findings are drawn from an online survey of 120 students and conversations with club organisers.\n\nOverview of current clubs\nThere are currently fourteen clubs, covering sport, music, drama, debating and languages. The football and music clubs are thriving, with waiting lists for new members. By contrast, the language and chess clubs struggle to attract more than a handful of students each week.\n\nKey problems\nThree main obstacles emerged. Firstly, many students consider the clubs too formal; several respondents described them as "an extra lesson" rather than a social activity. Secondly, there is little variety for students who are not interested in sport or performing arts. Thirdly, club information is spread across noticeboards, emails and social media, so students often miss announcements.\n\nRecommendations\nIn order to make clubs more appealing, organisers should be encouraged to include informal, social elements, such as games, trips or shared meals. The college could also introduce a simple process allowing students to start new clubs, perhaps requiring a minimum of eight interested members. Finally, it is recommended that all club information be gathered in a single online calendar.\n\nConclusion\nThe college clearly has a strong foundation of clubs, but several are failing to reach their potential. Implementing the measures above would require minimal expense and, in my view, would lead to a noticeable increase in student involvement.'),
 (L3,'Student clubs: evaluation and recommendations\n\nIntroduction\nAt the director\'s request, this report evaluates the college\'s extracurricular clubs, identifies factors limiting participation and puts forward a number of recommendations. It draws on a questionnaire completed by 96 students, attendance records for the past term and informal interviews with club leaders.\n\nStrengths\nThe college offers a respectable range of clubs, and several – most notably the debating society and the orchestra – have achieved considerable success in regional competitions. Members of these clubs spoke enthusiastically about the friendships and skills they had developed.\n\nAreas of concern\nDespite this, participation is uneven. Attendance records indicate that almost half of all club members belong to just three clubs, while others have dwindled to only a few regular attendees. Survey responses point to two underlying causes: a perception that clubs cater primarily to students who are already highly skilled, and a lack of clubs reflecting newer interests such as coding, sustainability or mental well-being.\n\nRecommendations\nIt would be advisable for established clubs to introduce beginner sessions, making it clear that no prior experience is required. In addition, the college should invite proposals for new clubs at the start of each academic year, supporting promising ideas with a small start-up budget. Finally, a brief "taster week", during which students could attend any club without commitment, would lower the barrier to joining.\n\nConclusion\nWhile the existing clubs have much to be proud of, they currently serve a relatively narrow group of students. If adopted, the measures outlined above should broaden participation significantly at minimal cost.')],
'c1w4':[(L2,'Proposal: a riverside music and arts festival\n\nAim\nThe purpose of this proposal is to suggest a festival that would celebrate local talent, bring residents of all ages together and attract visitors to the town.\n\nFestival content\nI propose an annual weekend festival along the riverbank each June. The main attraction would be two outdoor stages featuring local bands, choirs and school orchestras, with a well-known headline act on the Saturday evening. Alongside the music, an arts trail could display work by local painters and photographers in shop windows throughout the town centre. For families, a children\'s area with face painting, storytelling and craft workshops would ensure the event appealed to all ages.\n\nBenefits to the town\nSuch a festival would provide a valuable platform for local performers, many of whom have few opportunities to perform in public. Shops, cafés and hotels would benefit from increased trade, and the arts trail in particular would encourage visitors to explore the centre rather than remaining by the river.\n\nPossible difficulties\nThe most significant concerns are likely to be noise and litter. To address these, music could finish at 10.30 p.m., and volunteers from local schools and clubs could be recruited to keep the site clean in exchange for a donation to their organisation. Funding could be secured through sponsorship from local businesses and a modest charge for the headline concert only.\n\nConclusion\nA riverside festival of this kind would be relatively inexpensive to organise and would give the town an event to be genuinely proud of.'),
 (L3,'Proposal for a "Festival of Lights" in the old town\n\nIntroduction\nIn response to the council\'s invitation, this proposal sets out plans for an annual winter festival which, I believe, would enliven the town during its quietest months and strengthen community ties.\n\nThe concept\nTaking place over the first weekend of December, the festival would transform the historic streets of the old town with light installations created by local artists, students and community groups. Each evening, a lantern procession – led by children from local schools – would wind its way to the main square, where a programme of acoustic music and storytelling would follow. Stalls selling hot food and handmade crafts would line the route, with priority given to local producers.\n\nAnticipated benefits\nUnlike a summer festival, which would compete with countless similar events, a winter festival would fill a genuine gap, attracting visitors at a time when local businesses most need custom. Furthermore, the collaborative nature of the installations would involve hundreds of residents in the months leading up to the event, fostering a strong sense of ownership.\n\nAddressing potential concerns\nSafety on narrow, crowded streets would naturally be a priority; a one-way pedestrian route and clearly marked exits would help to manage crowds. Concerns about cost could be mitigated by using energy-efficient lighting and by seeking sponsorship from local firms, who would gain considerable visibility.\n\nConclusion\nThe Festival of Lights would offer a distinctive, inclusive celebration of the town\'s heritage and creativity. I would urge the council to give it serious consideration.')],
'c1w5':[(L2,'Dear Editor,\n\nI was dismayed to read last week\'s article calling for the closure of our town library. As someone who has used the library regularly for over twenty years, I would like to offer a rather different perspective.\n\nThe article suggests that, because information is now available online, libraries have become obsolete. This argument fundamentally misunderstands what a library offers. Online information is abundant, but it is also unreliable and often hidden behind paywalls. Librarians help people of all ages find trustworthy sources – a skill that has arguably never been more important.\n\nMoreover, the library serves as a lifeline for groups who are easily overlooked. Job seekers without home computers use it to apply for work; parents bring toddlers to free rhyme-time sessions; and students who lack a quiet place at home rely on it to study for their exams. Closing it would hit precisely those residents who can least afford alternatives.\n\nThe writer also claims that the money could be spent on "more urgent needs". Yet the library\'s running costs amount to a very small proportion of the council\'s budget, and the savings would be modest compared with the long-term costs to education and community life.\n\nIf the council is genuinely concerned about value for money, it should consider ways of making better use of the building, for instance by sharing it with other public services, rather than closing it altogether.\n\nI hope the council will listen to the many residents who value this vital service.\n\nYours faithfully,\nMehmet Yıldız'),
 (L3,'Dear Editor,\n\nYour recent article describing the town library as "an expensive luxury" has prompted considerable debate in our household, and I feel compelled to respond.\n\nThe author\'s central claim – that the internet has rendered libraries redundant – rests on a misconception. A library is not merely a warehouse for books, any more than a school is merely a building containing teachers. It is a public space in the truest sense: free, open to all and requiring no justification for one\'s presence. In an age when almost every other public place expects us to spend money, its value can hardly be overstated.\n\nConsider who would bear the cost of closure. Not those of us with fast broadband and shelves of books at home, but elderly residents who rely on the library for company and assistance with online forms, newcomers attending its language classes, and teenagers for whom it offers the only quiet place to study. To describe such provision as a luxury is, frankly, to misunderstand who it serves.\n\nI do not dispute that the council faces difficult financial choices. However, closing a library is a decision that is easy to make and almost impossible to reverse. Once the building is sold and the collection dispersed, it will not return when finances improve.\n\nI would therefore urge the council to explore alternatives – partnerships with local businesses, shared use of the building, volunteer support – before taking such an irreversible step.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAyşe Demir')],
}
